  "Number": 46,
  "Question": "For the equation \\(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}=\\dfrac{N+1}{N}\\) to be true where \\(N\\) is positive, \\(x\\) can have:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "any positive value less than \\(1\\)",
    "B": "any value less than \\(1\\)",
    "C": "the value zero only",
    "D": "any non-negative value",
    "E": "any value"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Solving for \\(x\\) will show how the condition \\(N>0\\) restricts its possible values. The original equation requires \\(x\\ne1\\), and \\(N>0\\) already ensures \\(N\\ne0\\). We may therefore multiply by \\(N(1-x)\\):\n\\[\nN(1+x)=(N+1)(1-x).\n\\]\nExpanding and collecting the terms containing \\(x\\) gives\n\\[\n(2N+1)x=1,\n\\qquad x=\\frac{1}{2N+1}.\n\\]\nSince \\(N>0\\), the denominator is greater than \\(1\\), so \\(0<x<1\\).\n\nWe must also show that every value in this interval is possible. Given any \\(0<x<1\\), choose\n\\[\nN=\\frac{1-x}{2x}>0.\n\\]\nThen both original denominators are nonzero, and\n\\[\n\\frac{N+1}{N}=\\frac{1+x}{1-x}.\n\\]\nThus the possible values are exactly the positive numbers less than \\(1\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
